# The UNCOVERED ceiling. conformance/gates fails if the ledger's UNCOVERED count RISES above it.
#
# Why a checked-in number and not just a printed one: a printed count is something you can
# stop reading. This one has to be edited by hand, in a commit, which makes progress and
# regression both visible in the diff — and makes "the corpus quietly shrank" impossible,
# because deleting a test raises the count and breaks the build.
#
# LOWER it whenever coverage lands.
#
# RAISE it only when the count rose because hidden work became VISIBLE — a section moving out
# of N/A into UNCOVERED. Never to accommodate a test that was deleted, a marker that was
# dropped, or a section reclassified out of COVERED: those mean coverage was LOST, and each
# wants explaining rather than absorbing. The distinction is the whole point, so a raise
# carries its reason here.
#
# 321 -> 319 (this commit): §4.4 and §4.5.3, the last two rows of chapter 4, by giving the
# generator the one capability it lacked. Both sections are ABOUT declarations — §4.5.3 names
# seven kinds of variable and three of them (class variable, instance variable, constructor
# parameter) cannot be introduced by any statement, and §4.4's list of positions a type name
# may occupy is half field/parameter/result positions. A Snippet renders an expression or a
# statement, so neither section was reachable, and I twice wrote that down as a blocker instead
# of removing it. `Declaring` is the removal: a snippet may also carry whole top-level type
# declarations, which Emit hoists to file scope and dedupes by text.
#
# The two are covered by ENUMERATION, not by sampling: Sn4Kinds sums one contribution per kind
# to 127, so a kind that silently failed to be created changes the total, and Sn4Positions uses
# a single named type in every position §4.4 lists.
#
# 325 -> 321 (previous, and recorded late — the number moved in 521df0b without this entry):
# chapters 1 and 2, plus §4.5. All three had been N/A on a chapter-level hypothesis the plan
# explicitly says does not authorise skipping a section; read against the sections it held for
# none of them. §1.1 prints a program AND its output, §2.2's "white space and comments are
# discarded" is a claim about meaning, and §2.4's bracket notation applied to ForStatement is
# the claim that all eight `for` forms are legal Java.
#
# 368 -> 325 (previous): NOT new tests — coverage that already existed and was not being
# counted. conformance/gen's snippet libraries claim their sections through the `// JLS <n>`
# markers Emit writes into each generated case, but nothing ran the generator, so
# conformance/generated was empty and the join read no markers. Wiring run-generated.sh into
# the gate turned 43 already-written sections from UNCOVERED into COVERED.
#
# It also surfaced two over-claims the join had never been in a position to see: a snippet
# naming §20.12.47, which exists in the body but not in the ToC the inventory is transcribed
# from, and one naming the parents §15.13/§15.14 instead of the four operator leaves it
# actually exercises — a parent claim marks its whole subtree covered on one child.
#
# 398 -> 368 (previous): all thirty of chapter 14's remaining sections, by
# conformance/jls/Ch14.java plus six conformance/reject cases. Statements are where the DDCG's
# destination-driven lowering lives, so these are the shapes the backend has to frame right —
# loop headers, join labels, break/continue targets, finally on every exit path — and they are
# the reason §14.19's reachability rules are compile-time errors rather than assertions.
#
# 409 -> 398 (previous): eleven of chapter 11's fourteen sections, by conformance/jls/Ch11.java.
# §11.4 needed a compiler fix to land, not a weaker test: its worked output prints
# ArithmeticException WITH "/ by zero", and the divide guard used throw_new_noarg, so
# getMessage() was null. The guard now builds `new ArithmeticException(new String(<pooled
# char[]>))` through throw_new_msg — which only became expressible once the constant-data pool
# took raw bytes, since `rule string_lit` panics by design and a synthesized guard has no AST
# literal for sema to desugar.
